{"success": true, "name": "reasoning:arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md", "content": "# Panel extraction and output-dimension validation

When demonstrations have unusually wide/tall inputs and much smaller outputs, inspect the entire layout before assuming a cellwise transformation. Look for a separator line/column and distinct panels on either side. Across demos, compare output dimensions to the panel dimensions: if the output consistently matches one panel, the task likely extracts or transforms that panel while discarding the other, separator, or framing. Normalize colors by role and test candidate panel selection against every example.

After inferring the selected panel, determine its transformation independently from the discarded region. Do not let random-looking cells elsewhere in the input distract from a compact output panel. Apply the same spatial crop/projection to the test input, then map colors using the demos. Validate the resulting row count and each row width against the inferred output dimensions before emitting JSON. Incorrect feedback may stem from a wrong selection rule, a wrong transformation, or simply malformed output dimensions; distinguish these failure classes before spending another attempt.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "reasoning:arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md", "content": "# Protocol discipline and avoiding speculative fill rules

Continual ARC tasks require one valid action on every active turn, even after an interruption. A prose status report is not a valid substitute; when the instance remains active, choose among submit, request_demos, or finish according to the live counts and end-state rules. Do not make unrelated tool calls in the evaluator loop: they consume interaction opportunities without providing puzzle evidence.

After each demo, compare all changed and preserved cells before taking another costly action. In fill/color tasks, do not assume the output is a filled bounding rectangle or the complement of a flood-filled exterior. Those are competing hypotheses that need to explain every demo, including holes, boundaries, marker cells, and untouched regions. Consider directional propagation or selectively filled regions only when supported by cellwise evidence. An incorrect submission should prompt a meaningful rule revision, not a cosmetic redraw.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md"}
